cat > "$SIM_DIR/discussion.json" << 'DISCUSSION_EOF'
{
  "assessment_mode": "sub-agent",
  "assessment_mode_intentional": true,
  "partner_verdicts": [
    {
      "partner": "visionary",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Compelling demand signal and disruptive team pedigree, but missing a 10-year category narrative and a clear 'why now' macro catalyst. The portfolio conflict situation needs resolution first. Not a pass — the founders may have the insight I'm looking for, but they haven't shown it yet."
    },
    {
      "partner": "operator",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Strong unit economics and sustained growth velocity are real execution signals, but the GTM is a black box. Two technical founders in an inherently high-touch SMB market with no commercial hire is a structural gap. Need cohort data and channel attribution before committing."
    },
    {
      "partner": "analyst",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "CAC payback of ~4.5 months and 80% gross margins are top-quartile for seed SaaS. The surface unit economics look excellent. However, no churn data, no burn rate, no headcount disclosed — the $16M pre-money is defensible at 18% MoM growth but requires significant information before the financial case can be validated."
    }
  ],
  "debate_sections": [
    {
      "topic": "The Demand Signal: Real or Ephemeral?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I want to start with what I find genuinely exciting: 18% MoM over four consecutive months in SMB field service. This market is famously resistant to software adoption — HVAC techs don't wake up in the morning thinking 'I should upgrade my SaaS stack.' The fact that 120 of them have opened their wallets for Cadence tells me there is a real product insight here. The ex-Notion background explains it: these founders know how to build products that feel different from enterprise software. That's the wedge."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I don't disagree with the signal, but I need to understand what's generating it. Is this PLG — customers discovering and activating on their own? Or is this founder-led sales where they're personally closing every deal? Those two things look identical at 120 customers and completely diverge at 500. Walk me through the last 20 customer acquisitions: who found who, what closed the deal, how long did it take?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "And layered on top of the Operator's question: what happens after month 3? The four-month growth window is too short to know if we're watching a compounding growth machine or a growing pipeline filling a leaky bucket. If monthly gross churn is 3%, the net growth looks like 15%. If it's 5%, you're already underwater on the cohorts. I cannot sign off on a conviction view without at minimum a 6-month cohort retention curve."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Fair — but let's not let the perfect be the enemy of the good. At 120 customers and $40K MRR, asking for 12 months of cohort data is a Series A bar, not a seed bar. What I care about is whether the customers who are using it love it. One strong NPS number or a handful of customer quotes would move me more at this stage than cohort tables."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I actually disagree here. SMB SaaS churn is the original sin of the category. Field service SMBs are the hardest cohort to retain — seasonality, cash flow volatility, owner-operated businesses that go dark, staff turnover. If month-6 retention is below 70%, this business requires a growth rate above 20% just to maintain flat MRR. That's not a seed-stage 'we'll figure it out later' issue. That's a question we need answered now, even if the data is thin."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "The Portfolio Conflict: Blocking or Manageable?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "This is the room's elephant. We have active positions in Jobber and HouseCall Pro. Both are direct field-service SaaS companies serving SMB home service operators — essentially the same ICP as Cadence. The conflict check flagged both as blocking severity. Before we go further, someone needs to tell me how we're thinking about this."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "It depends entirely on how Cadence is differentiated. If Cadence is 'Jobber but better UI,' that's a blocking conflict and we stop here. But if Cadence has a structurally different approach — say, AI-native dispatch optimization, or a vertical wedge in a segment Jobber doesn't prioritize, or a fundamentally different pricing model — then it's a different product serving the same broad market, which is more like customer overlap than direct conflict. We need the founders to make that case compellingly."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "This is actually the version of the conversation I find most interesting. Jobber and HouseCall Pro are both built on the same architectural assumption: you manage jobs in a system of record. What if Cadence's thesis is that AI-native real-time dispatch is a different paradigm entirely — closer to Uber's dispatch engine than a traditional SaaS job board? That would be a 'why now' argument I could get behind. The LLM-native operations thesis is real. But I haven't heard these founders make that case, and that's my biggest open question."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "Regardless of how differentiated the product is, our legal team needs to review our obligations to Jobber and HouseCall Pro before we proceed. That's non-negotiable. If they're in the same fund, we have information barriers and potential board duty conflicts that have to be cleared. This isn't a philosophical debate — it's process."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "Agreed. Legal clearance is table stakes. But I want to come back to the Operator's framing: what I'm really asking is whether these founders understand their competitive position well enough to articulate a wedge. If they can clearly explain why a Jobber customer would switch to Cadence — not just 'better UX' but a structural reason — then the conflict becomes manageable. If they can't, then we have two problems: the conflict and the competitive strategy."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "The Team Gap: Two Technical Founders in a High-Touch Market",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "This is the concern that keeps me up at night on this deal. Field service operators — HVAC techs, plumbers, landscapers — are not Notion users. They don't discover software through Product Hunt or through a self-serve onboarding flow. They talk to other contractors at the supply house, they get recommendations from their industry association, they respond to outbound calls. The ex-Stripe, ex-Notion skillset is perfect for building a product that's technically excellent and delightful to use. It may be entirely wrong for reaching the buyer."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I'd push back on the framing. The ex-Notion background is specifically relevant because Notion proved you could acquire a business-critical workflow tool through bottoms-up PLG without a traditional sales force. The question is whether the field service market is bottoms-up accessible. And there's a plausible argument that it is — operators are increasingly digitally native, younger tradespeople are adopting smartphone-first tools, and platforms like Thumbtack and Angi have conditioned the market to discover tools online. But this is a hypothesis, not a fact."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The data I'd want to see is channel attribution for the 120 customers. If 70% came from organic search or referral with no sales involvement, the Visionary's PLG hypothesis looks compelling. If 80% came from founder-dialed outbound, we have a different conversation. The truth is in the acquisition channel mix, and we don't have it."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "And when we do see it, I want to understand what the first commercial hire looks like. Is it a VP Sales from a Jobber or ServiceTitan background who knows how to sell to trades? Or are they planning to replicate a PLG motion that has never been proven in this vertical at scale? The first sales hire in a technical founding team often determines whether the GTM transition works or breaks the culture. I'd want to understand their thinking on that hire before we close."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "The Price: 33x ARR at Seed — Defensible?",
      "exchanges": [
